<p>A U.S. Navy submarine fired a torpedo that sank the Iranian warship IRIS Dena in international waters in the Indian Ocean off Sri Lanka on March 4, expanding the fighting beyond the Middle East. Sri Lanka’s navy said it recovered 87 bodies and rescued 32 survivors; some were treated at a hospital in Galle. U.S. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth said the strike was the first time since World War II that the U.S. sank an enemy ship with a torpedo. Iranian officials condemned the action and warned of consequences. The incident has also sharpened scrutiny of Washington’s aims at sea. Reporting cited U.S. military claims of broader operations against Iran’s naval presence, while some former U.S. officials questioned whether a goal of eliminating Iran’s military could breach international law. Even as tensions rise, Sri Lanka’s rescue and medical response has been a small reminder of how quickly lifesaving cooperation can happen at sea.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/-H39gvVbbpVPZELRnbojhyupO5d6CzEK53vvvTwHWEtk-VWWWWH3cPHgS4LlJGsDX828PZlJa67LmaYFGw2_rbAha2DozJcdcJQ_Pw3UlVdGXqBp3CTcf3K5D_6gdHFqJBUEY7JmY_guitPJKIa0P1_9y0ck4r5SdjAJt6PTj0HYuhNDV9zEwUTwj2m-oJdRFaMG-gNd9HYX4x0e9euzCIqasIEHgoEaX7sqz1QFwCNst3Si5GdQX7G5wk3M08uATwGmgNYT7gBKYt9zMBDbLJBoR1qvh2cf1UTSdx3LzGJJFLrFQt37ZrFaSzcYKyPTnm10G60kUm08mIHAv8lwShmE7vZyXOyOmpMIBiDRK8oruPmdijnysKJSaGNCwqHgllcgiW_QvlUp0BaUA8NDwjhgPWKYdmAr1Q_5wOASMUj7GBAr1A7tEeyEFK-dkoP7J7mAM_eG-Iw0Q_ps8bMPGZiMw5PtYerCp-ihG9WLeuzR9GHxtk96jHM' alt='Aftermath of the Iranian warship sinking off Sri Lanka, as reported by The Globe and Mail.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Distress call and medical intake: Sri Lankan authorities said military rescuers answered an early-morning distress call from the IRIS Dena and brought survivors and recovered bodies to Galle, including the National Hospital.</li><li>Operational footage: Outlets described black-and-white Pentagon video of the strike, including the moment an explosion hits before the vessel sinks.</li><li>Regional proximity: Commentary said the sinking near Sri Lanka pulls a major wartime incident into sea lanes around South Asia, heightening regional security concerns.</li><li>Broader naval campaign: A top U.S. officer said Iran’s major naval presence in the area of operations had been neutralized.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. Defense Department: Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth called the IRIS Dena one of Iran’s “prize ships,” and he framed the torpedo sinking as a major operational milestone as the Pentagon released strike video. (<a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/03/04/pentagon-video-shows-us-torpedo-attack-on-iranian-warship'>Euronews</a>)</li><li>Iran (Foreign Ministry): Iran’s foreign minister warned the U.S. would “bitterly regret” what he called the precedent set by the strike. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-israel-us-war-attacks-strikes-west-asia-lebanon-trump-netanyahu-live-updates/article70705745.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Sri Lanka Navy and health officials: Sri Lankan officials described a search-and-rescue and recovery operation at sea and said rescued Iranian sailors were taken to hospitals in Galle for treatment. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-submarine-torpedoes-iranian-warship-in-indian-ocean/a-76216943'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li><li>Former U.S. officials (as cited): Former U.S. officials questioned whether a goal of eliminating all of Iran’s military would breach international law. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/04/us-submarine-torpedo-iran-warship-sri-lanka-coast-pete-hegseth'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>SCMP analysis: The report framed the strike as part of a U.S. effort to curb Iran’s ability to disrupt maritime flows by targeting its navy. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3345499/irans-navy-crosshairs-warship-sinking-us-submarine-ups-game'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/